WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.330
An educational side hustle?

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.250
A pandemic-themed holiday song?

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.204
There's a lot you may not know about The Tonight
Show's Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:08.204 --> 00:00:12.769
Many comedians have dark pasts that fuel their
need for laughter, but Jimmy Fallon had it

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:14.960
pretty well growing up by all accounts.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.450
The future host of "The Tonight Show" was
born in New York City to happy parents Gloria

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:20.549
and James Fallon.

00:00:20.549 --> 00:00:24.699
As he'd later describe to Vanity Fair, his
childhood was picture perfect.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:25.699
He elaborated —

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:30.560
"My grandparents were so happy, everyone in
the family was funny, and there was no divorce,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.560
no unemployment."

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.190
Nevertheless, like many childhoods, Fallon's
was full of rules: his parents were strict

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.680
in terms of what he was allowed to watch and
where he was allowed to go.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.100
There was eventually an exception to that
rule, however: the NBC sketch comedy program

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.534
"Saturday Night Live."

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:48.410
As a teenager, Fallon became obsessed with
the show.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.290
He would stay up late every Saturday night
to watch it, a tradition he continues now

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.624
with his wife and children.

00:00:53.624 --> 00:00:58.884
Aside from "Saturday Night Live," Fallon was
an avid listener of "The Dr. Demento Show,"

00:00:58.884 --> 00:01:02.510
which introduced the world to comedy acts
like "Weird" Al Yankovic.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.950
Fallon's family was the perfect audience for
his own early attempts at comedy, including

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.729
impressions of Dana Carvey and Pee-Wee Herman.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:15.000
Soon enough, Fallon would be on his way to
becoming an ambitious new face on the comedy scene.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.880
Before Jimmy Fallon was fully committed to
his future as a comedian, his other job prospects

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.560
were influenced by other aspects of his upbringing.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.310
The Fallons were a family of devout Irish
Catholics, which led a young Jimmy to consider

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.399
becoming a priest.

00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:32.829
While many would feel inclined to do this
out of a deep respect for their religion,

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:36.549
Fallon had more of an interest in the pageantry
of the church, which he became acquainted

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:37.894
with as an altar boy.

00:01:37.894 --> 00:01:42.229
As Fallon later recalled, being an altar boy
was his first experience with being in front

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:46.950
of a crowd, but eventually it became clear
he was desperate for a little more validation.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:53.079
He echoed this idea on Mike Birbiglia's "Working
It Out" podcast in 2022, mentioning that being

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.640
a funny priest seemed much easier than being
a funny comedian.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.310
Still, Fallon's interest in comedy eventually
thrust him into the much higher stakes world

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:02.059
of stand-up.

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:06.299
Fallon performed stand-up comedy on the side
during his college years, often taking the

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:11.356
bus from The College of Saint Rose in Albany
to the famous Caroline's Comedy Club in Times Square.

00:02:11.356 --> 00:02:16.490
Though his early attempts at telling jokes
trended more towards Andy Kaufman-esque stunts,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.879
he found a small amount of success through
impressions, which guided him towards the

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:23.280
next stage of his burgeoning comedy career.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.561
"First up, Mr. John Travolta…”

00:02:27.561 --> 00:02:30.892
“Geezlikethanksforhavingmeisweartogodthesethingsaresoweirdoverhereright?"

00:02:32.702 --> 00:02:37.760
After dropping out of college in 1995, Jimmy
Fallon moved to Los Angeles with the goal

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.810
of pursuing comedy full-time — a prospect
many have tried, but few have successfully

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:43.813
been able to do.

00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:48.069
Nevertheless, his ambition was noticed by
Hollywood professionals, eventually gaining

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:51.260
him a manager and earning him his first few
dollars on stage.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:56.170
He enrolled in improv classes with The Groundlings,
a historic comedy school with alumni including

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.319
Conan O'Brien, Tyra Banks, and Dax Shepard.

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:02.989
It wasn't long before Fallon started to sneak
his way into film and TV.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:07.950
He landed his first TV gig with a guest appearance
as a photographer in the second season of

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:13.050
ABC's "Spin City," a late '90s sitcom starring
Michael J. Fox and Richard Kind.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:18.600
Believe it or not, Fallon's casting on "Spin
City" may be owed to a certain future Avenger.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.930
During a 2023 episode of "The Tonight Show,"
Fallon and Chris Evans discussed the fact

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.390
that, around the time that Fallon appeared
on "Spin City," Evans was an intern in the

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.390
casting office.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.390
"I think you may have cast me—"

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:30.700
"Did I give you your first job?!"

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:31.700
"Wow!

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:32.700
This is…

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.510
I feel like I owe my whole career to you!"

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.510
"Yeah, that's amazing!"

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:40.879
Still, performing on "SNL" would remain Fallon's
white whale for years to come.

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:45.160
Jimmy Fallon's determination to be a cast
member on "Saturday Night Live" didn't wane

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:50.320
during his time in Los Angeles, and his opportunity
to audition finally came when he was just

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.169
21 years old.

00:03:51.169 --> 00:03:55.300
He didn't impress the show's producers enough
to earn a spot on the cast or as a writer

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.909
— and for a moment, Fallon's childhood dream
seemed crushed.

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:02.720
While others would have taken this loss as
the end of their journey, Fallon kept his

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:03.343
chin up.

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:09.631
Over the next two years, he accrued more experience
and TV roles, including a pilot for The WB network.

00:04:09.631 --> 00:04:14.040
Fallon was deadly serious about his ambitions:
he recounted to Rolling Stone —

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.340
"I remember saying to myself, 'If I don't
make it on 'Saturday Night Live' before I'm

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.370
25, I'm going to kill myself.'"

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.774
Fortunately, things didn't come to that, as
Fallon got a second chance to audition in 1997.

00:04:25.774 --> 00:04:30.600
His winning bit was a parade of celebrity
impressions, which included Jerry Seinfeld,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.380
Chris Rock, and Adam Sandler, which pulled
a rare laugh from "SNL" producer Lorne Michaels.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:40.759
"So I was like, I was like — 'Yeah, my mom,
uh, wants me to go on this walkathon, and

00:04:40.759 --> 00:04:44.253
I keep telling her, you know, SHABADEDOO!'"

00:04:44.253 --> 00:04:48.263
Fallon joined the cast of Season 24 in 1998.

00:04:48.263 --> 00:04:53.160
After his audition, "Saturday Night Live"
head writer Tina Fey testified that Fallon

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.434
was truly prepared to be a part of the cast.

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:58.726
Fey recalled Fallon's audition to Vanity Fair,
saying —

00:04:58.726 --> 00:05:02.714
"He's one of two people I've ever seen who
was completely ready to be on the show."

00:05:02.714 --> 00:05:07.979
Luckily, Fey's hunch proved correct, as Fallon
quickly won over audiences with the same Adam

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:10.740
Sandler impression that won over Lorne Michaels.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.937
Seemingly overnight, Jimmy Fallon went from
struggling stand-up comic to the latest "SNL" star.

00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:20.900
He appeared in recurring sketches like The
Boston Teens with Rachel Dratch and had many

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:26.382
amusing instances of breaking character and
cracking up with fellow cast member Horatio Sanz.

00:05:26.382 --> 00:05:31.669
Fallon famously broke during the iconic "More
Cowbell" sketch, as well as one disastrous

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:36.667
edition of Debbie Downer at Disney World.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:42.782
"I can't have children!"

00:05:42.782 --> 00:05:48.550
Fallon's escapades, partially with Sanz, weren't
just limited to their on-air hijinks.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:53.930
Notably, the two took full advantage of "Saturday
Night Live's" culture of partying and alcohol,

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:58.879
staying out into the early hours of the morning
drinking and getting into occasional bar fights.

00:05:58.879 --> 00:06:00.378
Sanz later recounted to Vulture —

00:06:00.378 --> 00:06:05.520
"We definitely fed into the fact that being
out of control is good on that show."

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.001
"Saturday Night Live" quickly increased Jimmy
Fallon's celebrity profile, as the comedian

00:06:10.001 --> 00:06:14.714
was upgraded from featured player to main
cast member after only one season.

00:06:14.714 --> 00:06:20.020
Fallon later gained even more notoriety when
he began co-hosting "Weekend Update" alongside

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.398
Tina Fey during his third season on the show.

00:06:22.398 --> 00:06:27.560
Around the same time, Fallon played a minor
role in Cameron Crowe's 2000 film "Almost

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.000
Famous," as promotional manager Dennis Hope.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.240
Eventually, Fallon ended up being chosen to
host various award shows for MTV over the

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.270
years, including the MTV Movie Awards and
the Video Music Awards.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:45.060
While hosting the former in 2005, Fallon first
encountered a trio of writers and sketch comedians

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:51.050
known as The Lonely Island, consisting of
Andy Samberg, Akiva Schaffer, and Jorma Taccone.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.830
Their unique sense of humor caught Fallon's
attention.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:58.210
Fallon ended up recommending the trio's online
content to the producers of "Saturday Night

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.649
Live," and before long, Lorne Michaels invited
them to audition.

00:07:01.649 --> 00:07:06.300
Samberg joined the show as a cast member with
Jorma and Akiva as writers, and the three

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.364
completely changed "SNL" for the digital age.

00:07:09.364 --> 00:07:13.520
Even after each of them moved on from the
show, the members of The Lonely Island have

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.240
appeared on "The Tonight Show" on occasion.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.061
Jimmy Fallon eventually departed from "Saturday
Night Live" following Season 29 in 2004, after

00:07:22.061 --> 00:07:26.829
which he was replaced as Tina Fey's "Weekend
Update" co-anchor by Amy Poehler.

00:07:26.829 --> 00:07:30.960
Fortunately, the future looked bright for
Fallon, as he had just starred in his first

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.190
lead film role alongside Queen Latifah in
"Taxi."

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:38.639
However, the film ended up doing poorly at
the box office when it was released that same

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:41.823
year, despite Fallon's performance charming
some reviewers.

00:07:41.823 --> 00:07:46.979
Fallon's second chance came with a role he
seemed better suited for in the 2005 romantic

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:49.909
comedy "Fever Pitch" opposite Drew Barrymore.

00:07:49.909 --> 00:07:55.139
The film centers on the romance between Fallon's
character, a devout Boston Red Sox fanatic,

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:58.590
and Barrymore's, a workaholic who doesn't
understand his obsession.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:03.370
However, "Fever Pitch" also saw a lackluster
return at the box office, while reviews from

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:07.033
critics and viewers were also generally lukewarm.

00:08:07.033 --> 00:08:09.830
"Are you saying that she's out of my league?!"

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.933
"She's bringing some serious heat, man.

00:08:11.933 --> 00:08:13.585
I don't know if you got the bat speed."

00:08:13.585 --> 00:08:14.735
"Oh, I got the bat speed."

00:08:15.037 --> 00:08:20.030
Following the failure of "Fever Pitch," Fallon
hit a surprising dead end in his career.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.599
Once film offers stopped rolling in, Fallon
decided to move back to New York City, eventually

00:08:24.599 --> 00:08:28.960
hitting a depressive period with no real insight
on how he'd go forward.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.300
However, all he ended up having to do was
wait for an old mentor to come knocking with

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.323
the opportunity of a lifetime.

00:08:35.323 --> 00:08:40.493
Even though it left his career at a standstill,
Fallon has a lot to thank "Fever Pitch" for.

00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:45.540
Through his work on the film, he ended up
falling in love with Nancy Juvonen, the co-founder

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.439
of Drew Barrymore's production company Flower
Films.

00:08:48.439 --> 00:08:52.910
Fallon and Juvonen later recounted the specifics
of their first introduction to each other

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:56.980
during an episode of "The Tonight Show: At
Home Edition," when he produced episodes of

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:01.129
the show with his family during the initial
COVID-19 lockdowns in 2020.

00:09:01.129 --> 00:09:06.090
On a walk outside their home, Juvonen recounted
how they first met at "Saturday Night Live"

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.839
when Barrymore was hosting, and Fallon eagerly
introduced himself to her.

00:09:09.839 --> 00:09:15.149
Juvonen added that she later developed a crush
on Fallon on the set of "Fever Pitch," after

00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:18.070
a producer brought their kids to set and she
noticed him playing with them.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:22.230
"I think that's when my heart just sort of…
melted even more."

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:23.230
"Awww.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:24.759
I remember those kids, oh they were so cute."

00:09:24.759 --> 00:09:25.759
"Yeah!"

00:09:25.985 --> 00:09:30.160
The two got married in 2007 and currently
live in New York with their two daughters,

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:33.321
who were born in 2013 and 2014.

00:09:33.321 --> 00:09:38.420
Fallon's wedding to Juvonen wasn't without
hijinks, as one would expect from a comedian's

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:39.420
wedding.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.380
As he later recounted on "The Drew Barrymore
Show" in 2020, his wedding was crashed by

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:47.630
none other than Kermit the Frog after the
videographer heard their wedding song would

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:49.250
be "The Rainbow Connection."

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:54.562
Although it was a perfect wedding regardless,
Kermit made it into every shot of the wedding video.

00:09:54.562 --> 00:09:59.329
Following Jimmy Fallon's career slump after
his failure in the world of movies, Lorne

00:09:59.329 --> 00:10:03.600
Michaels made good on a promise he made to
Fallon shortly before the comedian left "Saturday

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:04.600
Night Live."

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.019
At the time, Michaels and Tina Fey both suggested
that Fallon could be a good replacement for

00:10:09.019 --> 00:10:13.850
Conan O'Brien on "Late Night" when he eventually
took over "The Tonight Show" from Jay Leno.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:18.920
However, Michaels probably didn't expect that
recommending Fallon for the job in 2007 would

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.470
result in conflict with NBC executives.

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:23.120
As Fallon later told GQ —

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.410
"NBC was like, 'This is going to flop.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:27.318
This is going to be like Chevy Chase’s show.'"

00:10:27.318 --> 00:10:32.040
Michaels remained supportive of Fallon despite
the comedian's failure as a film star.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:35.660
Nevertheless, those executives were proven
right when "Late Night with Jimmy Fallon"

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:40.560
debuted in 2009, as some critics considered
the host a step down from Conan.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.800
However, Fallon slowly began to win over audiences,
especially as public behind-the-scenes turmoil

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:48.220
hit "The Tonight Show with Conan O'Brien."

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:53.209
The relatively drama-free "Late Night with
Jimmy Fallon" became a sanctuary for upbeat,

00:10:53.209 --> 00:10:54.053
positive comedy.

00:10:54.053 --> 00:10:59.420
Fallon's success on late night TV was, inevitably,
all thanks to Lorne's confidence, as he had

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:03.269
become a mentor figure for the comedian during
his six years at "SNL."

00:11:03.269 --> 00:11:07.850
Fortunately, Fallon lucked out with perhaps
the best house band in all of late night:

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:12.040
The Roots, who made the show something truly
worth watching.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.630
Fallon taped "Late Night" at 30 Rockefeller
Center, where he continued his talk show tenure

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:20.620
after inheriting "The Tonight Show" from Jay
Leno in 2014.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:25.290
This also kept him in close proximity to "Saturday
Night Live," where he has returned to host

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.290
three times so far over the years.

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:32.150
The comedian also made an appearance alongside
Justin Timberlake in the sketch series' 40th

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:37.240
anniversary special in 2015, which gathered
a who's who of Hollywood icons to celebrate

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:41.410
the show with reunion sketches, tributes,
and musical performances.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.900
The most memorable part of the night, however,
took place after the cameras stopped rolling.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:50.709
The cast, crew, and audience of "SNL 40" gathered
at the Plaza Hotel for what would become the

00:11:50.709 --> 00:11:55.050
most legendary after-party in the history
of "Saturday Night Live."

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:59.100
Fallon recounted the event the next night
on "The Tonight Show," revealing that there

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:04.120
was no scheduled musical performer, with the
party instead arranged to have an empty stage

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.338
and instruments for anyone to jam.

00:12:06.338 --> 00:12:11.270
After unintentionally accepting duties as
the party's emcee, Fallon roped in all sorts

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:12.600
of musical performers.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.250
Taylor Swift, Paul McCartney and Michael Bolton
all played their hits, but the true highlight

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:23.149
of the night came when Prince played "Let's
Go Crazy" to a starstruck audience of A-listers.

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:27.660
While his tenure on "The Tonight Show" has
mostly been a good time, Jimmy Fallon also

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.612
had to host while dealing with tragedy in
his personal life.

00:12:30.612 --> 00:12:36.100
In 2017, the talk show host canceled a week
of shows when his mother Gloria suddenly passed

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:40.029
away — and his first episode back was a
rollercoaster of emotions.

00:12:40.029 --> 00:12:43.209
"She would squeeze my hand three times and
say 'I love you.'

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:46.019
And I would squeeze back, 'I love you too.'"

00:12:46.019 --> 00:12:50.180
Taylor Swift was in town to perform on "Saturday
Night Live," and she agreed to be the musical

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:52.970
guest at the last minute in support of Fallon.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:58.029
The moment was made even more special when
Swift came on to perform the ballad "New Year's

00:12:58.029 --> 00:13:02.260
Day" off her 2017 album "Reputation," which
contains the line —

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:05.230
"You squeeze my hand three times in the back
of the taxi."

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:09.470
In a Twitter thread posted shortly after the
episode, "The Tonight Show" producer Mike

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:14.250
DiCenzo revealed that, since the album had
just been released days earlier, everyone

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.510
was shocked to hear the line that paralleled
Fallon's story.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:22.029
The host ended up in tears, and when the song
ended he gave Swift a heartfelt embrace.

00:13:22.029 --> 00:13:26.769
The song's producer, Jack Antonoff — a friend
of Fallon and frequent collaborator of Swift

00:13:26.769 --> 00:13:31.300
— also tweeted praise for the performance
and moment the next day.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:36.130
The COVID-19 pandemic derailed many things,
including the world of late-night TV.

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:40.459
Jimmy kFallon embraced his time in quarantine
by filming episodes of "The Tonight Show"

00:13:40.459 --> 00:13:44.990
in his home with the help of his family, which
made for a charming escape from the grim state

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:45.814
of the world.

00:13:45.814 --> 00:13:50.980
However, as the pandemic continued into 2021,
Fallon decided he wanted to do a little more

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.938
to help people escape their struggles.

00:13:52.938 --> 00:13:57.850
Recorded at New York City's Electric Lady
Studios, Fallon released a collaboration with

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.570
Ariana Grande and Megan Thee Stallion titled
"It Was A...

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:02.549
(Masked Christmas)."

00:14:02.549 --> 00:14:06.700
Fallon described his intent behind the song
to the Associated Press, saying —

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:10.860
"I wanted to write something reflecting on
how tough it was for everyone last year during

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:13.180
the holidays and that it's gonna get better."

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:18.350
The song covers relatable topics such as getting
booster vaccines, staying masked in public,

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:20.532
and participating in family Zoom sessions.

00:14:20.532 --> 00:14:25.240
"It Was A ... (Masked Christmas)" is only
the most recent in a long history of Christmas-themed

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.509
singles from Fallon.

00:14:26.509 --> 00:14:30.990
In the past, he duetted with the legendary
Dolly Parton for a cover of Mariah Carey's

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:35.949
"All I Want For Christmas is You," and he
also performed Paul McCartney's iconic "Wonderful

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:41.627
Christmastime" with the former Beatle, The
Roots, and the cast of "Sing" back in 2016.

00:14:41.627 --> 00:14:46.790
However, this 2021 tune was his first original
Christmas classic.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:51.579
Aside from hosting "The Tonight Show" since
2014, Jimmy Fallon has also made a career

00:14:51.579 --> 00:14:54.184
for himself as an author of children's books.

00:14:54.184 --> 00:14:59.899
This journey started for Fallon in 2015, when
he was inspired by recent fatherhood to write

00:14:59.899 --> 00:15:02.130
"Your Baby's First Word Will Be Dada."

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:07.290
In an interview with NPR, Fallon described
the book's premise as an act of selfishness,

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.459
explaining how he tried to trick his daughter
into saying the word "Dada."

00:15:10.459 --> 00:15:11.283
He joked —

00:15:11.283 --> 00:15:13.389
"I would call everything in the house 'dada.'"

00:15:13.389 --> 00:15:17.579
Fallon continued to regularly release children's
books over the years, all inspired by his

00:15:17.579 --> 00:15:19.220
experiences as a parent.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:25.009
He even channeled his love for Christmas into
a 2020 book titled "5 More Sleeps 'Til Christmas."

00:15:25.009 --> 00:15:30.329
However, his most adventurous experience came
in 2022, when he collaborated with none other

00:15:30.329 --> 00:15:35.779
than Jennifer Lopez on a children's book titled
"Con Pollo: A Bilingual Playtime Adventure."

00:15:35.779 --> 00:15:40.060
This team effort aims to teach kids Spanish
with the help of a lovable chicken amigo.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:41.286
"Eating chicken."

00:15:41.286 --> 00:15:43.375
"No, you're not eating the chicken in this.

00:15:43.375 --> 00:15:44.779
This — you're friends with the chicken."

00:15:44.779 --> 00:15:45.628
"Your friends eat the chicken."

00:15:45.628 --> 00:15:47.590
"No one eats the chicken."

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:52.380
Though the team-up between Fallon and Lopez
may seem random, it was something they both

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.239
had wanted to do for a long time.

00:15:54.239 --> 00:15:58.270
Fallon told People that approaching Lopez
to write the book with him was inspired by

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:00.460
his respect for her as a mother, saying —

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:04.290
"One of the many things I love about her is
how great of a mom she is."

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:09.180
What started for Fallon as a way to channel
his parenting anxieties has now become a successful

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:11.000
side venture for the comic.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:16.290
By 2023, Jimmy Fallon had accomplished a lot
in his career, much of which he probably never

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.979
anticipated when he was just a struggling
actor.

00:16:18.979 --> 00:16:23.079
While the comedian has plenty to look forward
to in his future, one bucket list item for

00:16:23.079 --> 00:16:25.569
him has recently been crossed off.

00:16:25.569 --> 00:16:31.199
In 2022, Fallon invited Cameron Crowe onto
"The Tonight Show" to promote a Broadway adaptation

00:16:31.199 --> 00:16:35.653
of his movie "Almost Famous," which Fallon
appeared in over 20 years earlier.

00:16:35.653 --> 00:16:40.829
On the show, Crowe personally invited Fallon
to reprise his role as Dennis Hope onstage

00:16:40.829 --> 00:16:46.348
at some point before "Almost Famous" closed
in January 2023, which Fallon accepted.

00:16:46.348 --> 00:16:51.079
Sadly, Fallon never made good on his promise
to Crowe, but he did make a special appearance

00:16:51.079 --> 00:16:54.649
during one of the last nights of the musical's
run on Broadway.

00:16:54.649 --> 00:16:59.019
After attending a performance with Lin-Manuel
Miranda and Crowe, Fallon was pulled on stage

00:16:59.019 --> 00:17:03.319
by one of the cast members during their ending
bows, which was met with roaring applause

00:17:03.319 --> 00:17:04.549
from the audience.

00:17:04.549 --> 00:17:08.361
It's clear that Fallon has come a long way
from where his career was at the turn of the

00:17:08.361 --> 00:17:11.940
century — and that he won't be going anywhere
anytime soon.

